I still don't know. This is a read-only way to find out that avoids the blind hour. Admin Activity audit logs live in `_Required`, which was never disabled, so they cover the whole day:

```bash
gcloud builds list --region=me-central2 --project=$P --filter="substitutions.TRIGGER_NAME=careernavigator-frontend-staging" --limit=8 --format="table(createTime,status,substitutions.SHORT_SHA)"
gcloud logging read 'logName:"cloudaudit.googleapis.com%2Factivity" AND (protoPayload.methodName:"compute.instances.insert" OR protoPayload.methodName:"compute.instances.delete") AND protoPayload.resourceName:"cn-frontend-mig-regional"' --project=$P --freshness=24h --format="table(timestamp,protoPayload.methodName,protoPayload.resourceName.basename())"
```
The principal is the same for rollouts and autoscaling, so I'm leaving that column out. Timing separates them:

| What you see | Meaning |
|---|---|
| server creations within a few minutes after a build's `createTime` | the extra servers come from rollouts, probably boot-time CPU on a new server pushing the group above 60% |
| creations with no build nearby | the autoscaler reacting to real load, which I'd then investigate separately |
| creations 30 seconds apart in pairs | one autoscaler step, as at 08:09 |

I haven't established which one it is. If it's the rollout pattern, the options are:

| | What | Cost | Complexity |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Lengthen the autoscaler cool-down** | Console → Instance groups → `cn-frontend-mig-regional` → Edit autoscaling → cool-down 60s to 300s | none | low |
| **B. Cap max at 2** | same screen | lower ceiling | low |
| **C. Observe** | accept up to 3 servers briefly after each deploy | up to 2 extra `e2-medium` for ~10 to 30 minutes per deploy | none |

I'd recommend A, then watch the next deploy. It stops boot-time CPU from being counted as load. Don't change anything until the timeline above is in.

